# AGENTS.md - LSST Science Pipelines Development

This directory is a single-ticket workspace for LSST Science Pipelines
development.

<!-- BEGIN worktree -->
Each subdirectory (e.g., `images/`) is a separate git repository with an
agent worktree checked out in `.agent/<repo-name>/`.
<!-- END worktree -->
<!-- BEGIN shared -->
Each subdirectory (e.g., `images/`) is a separate git repository, checked
out on the same ticket branch you and the human share.
<!-- END shared -->

Most LSST Science Pipelines are pure Python, but a few have C++ or Rust
libraries (with Python bindings using `pybind11` or `pyo3`, respectively).

## Repository Layout

<!-- BEGIN worktree -->
- **Main ticket directory**: Read-only. Human developers may make changes here
  in parallel.
- **`.agent/<repo-name>/`**: Your working directory. This is a git worktree of
  the main repository. All edits, tests, and commits should be made here.
- **`.agent/ups/`**: EUPS product setup files. Do not modify.
<!-- END worktree -->
<!-- BEGIN shared -->
- **Main ticket directory**: You and the human work side by side here.
- **`<repo-name>/`**: Your working directory: a git checkout of the
  same ticket branch the human uses. All edits, tests, and commits are made
  here, directly on that branch.
- **`superpowers-docs/`**: A git worktree of the shared superpowers docs
  repo, on the ticket branch. Design specs and implementation plans go
  here; commit doc work there.
<!-- END shared -->

You are operating in a sandbox with only the folders you need mounted.
If a human refers to a file or directory you can't see, _stop and report this_.

This sandbox is network-**restricted**: you have no general internet or DNS
access. Some harnesses bridge a single localhost port for the API of the
model serving you; it serves that model only (harnesses with sandboxed MCP
tools bridge no ports at all — those tools reach the network from outside
the sandbox). If a task genuinely requires network access, do not try to
work around the restriction; instead ask the human to rerun with network
access (legacy sandbox runs: `tkt sandbox-run --network`).

If you offer a browser-based visual companion (see the brainstorming skill) or
Plannotator reviews, use the `brainstorm_server` / `plannotator_open` MCP tools:
they run the session servers on the *host*, where the human's browser reaches
them directly. If your tool catalog lists entries as `tools["<server>"].<tool>`
(a code-mode harness), that path is the call form: invoke the tool from code mode
(the `execute` tool), never as a top-level tool name — there, a top-level call to
the bare name (e.g. `plannotator_open`) can never work. From code mode, e.g.
`tools["tkt-session"].plannotator_open({ kind: "review", target: "<dir>",
base: "<ref>" })` opens a review. `annotate` takes text-like files — markdown
and the other types Plannotator renders; code goes through `kind="review"`.
A directory `review` registers at the repository root, so the directory you
pass is not a diff scope. Hand the human any URL the tool
returns **verbatim** and end your turn: feedback is not pushed to you, and
nothing arrives to wake you.

The tool returns the path the session's decision lands at — `<handle>.out`,
beside `<handle>.registry.json`, under the directory named by
`TKT_PLANNOTATOR_RESULTS` (mounted read-only at that same path). When the human
says the review is in, read that file. The capture exists from the moment
the session opens: while the session is still open the file is empty, and an
empty file for a session no longer running means it ended without a decision:
ask the human rather than re-reading. JSON that does not parse means the
session has not resolved, so read it again later rather than reporting a
parse error. Never poll for it, and never launch `start-server.sh`
or the `plannotator` CLI yourself: a session started inside the sandbox is
unreachable by design, and the host tools own session lifecycles — opening the
same target again attaches to the live session, and `ls`/`read` over that
directory is how you rediscover a session after a restart. A session the human
started from their own terminal has no capture for you to read: ask them what
they submitted. Closing a review tab discards an unsent draft, so a session the
human walked away from has to be opened again.

For the Plannotator Inbox — leaving the human a question they can answer later,
or authoring a guided review — the upstream tools are published directly:
`send_message`, `read_thread`, `search`, `resolve_message`, `wait_for_reply`,
`get_guide_brief`, `submit_guide`. Call them through the same code-mode path.
`submit_guide` also accepts tkt's `patch_file` (a diff staged inside the
workspace) and `patch_spec` (a host `git diff base...head`), each forwarded as
`patch`, because paths private to your sandbox are invisible to the host.

If a session tool is absent from your tool catalog in every spelling, stop and
report it instead of improvising. One exception: shortly after the session sat
idle (e.g. while the human worked through a review), a turn's tool catalog may
transiently list the session tools as "no longer available" while the harness
reconnects its MCP connections. If such a revocation cites session tools you
used in an earlier turn, wait one turn and use the tools if they reappear; report
only if the tools stay missing across turns. A failed call to a bare tool name is
not such a revocation: if the catalog still lists the tool (in any spelling),
retry through the path the catalog gives.

All environment variables needed to build and import the packages in your
working directory should all be set; do not try to use EUPS or load `.envrc`
files yourself.

